  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1035 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nBy Thursday morning, the flattened upper level ridge is expected to \nsplit into two centers of high pressure as an upper trough \ntranslates into the NECONUS, while an additional trough begins to \ndive into the PacNW. Between these features, weak monsoonal moisture \nwrapping around the eastern periphery of the southwestern high, in \naddition to weaknesses within the ridge, will keep chances for \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms in the forecast \nthrough Friday morning. Additionally, a decrease in geopotential \nheights with the FA sandwiched between the two areas of high \npressure will help keep afternoon highs on Thursday on the cooler \nside in the 90s. Highs slightly warm Friday into the mid 90s to \nlower triple digits as we begin to see the ridge axis shift back \neast, closer to the region. \n\nAs the trough to our northwest translates east into the \nIntermountain West from the PacNW through the weekend, the upper \nhigh over the Desert Southwest will be kicked back over the region. \nIn response, surface flow will turn southwesterly in response to an \neastward moving lee surface low tracking east into the TX/OK \nPanhandle out ahead of the system. bringing breezy southwest winds \nand aiding in strong warm air advection into the region. Especially \nconsidering at the H7 level an area of high pressure will be parked \njust to our east with west-southwest flow also aiding in the uptick \nof thickness values across the region. With the center of the upper \nhigh and ridge axis positioned over the area, it will aid in a hot \nday Saturday, with record breaking heat possible at both Lubbock and \nChildress, while a Heat Advisory may be needed off the Caprock with \nhighs currently forecast around 100 to 104 degrees. Widespread \nprecipitation is not expected, though any weaknesses within the \nupper high tracking through an area of low to mid level moisture in \nplace may generate a few isolated showers and thunderstorms Saturday \nafternoon. The ridge begins departing east on Sunday, which will \nkeep highs from climbing further, though still hot in the lower to \nupper 90s which is still nearly 10 degrees above seasonal normals. \nAdditionally, flow aloft will begin to turn more southwesterly \nSunday, aiding in moisture transport around the H7 to H3 layer. \nbringing a slightly better chance for precipitation Sunday, though \nconfidence in this remains low. \n\nTurning to next week, ensembles and deterministic guidance as still \nhinting at a cooler and wetter period as na upper trough deepens and \nmoves into the High Plains from the Intermountain West. However, \nthis will depend entirely on how far south the associated cold front \ncan surge into the area. Guidance has been wish-washy over the last \nfew runs, with the GEFS still trending cooler than normal but coming \nin warmer than previous runs. Given the ongoing model spread, the \nplan for now is to maintain NBM temperatures and PoPs until frontal \ntiming and coverage becomes clearer.",
